Version 1 sync calls to third parties

Every request makes a sync HTTP call to the auth server
MeasurementsService calls authService.getUser(token) on every endpoint. That's an HTTP round-trip per request, blocking a Tomcat thread until the auth server responds. Under load, this serializes throughput.

 WebClient.block() is the worst of both worlds
AuthService uses reactive WebClient but calls .block() on every result. You pay the reactive machinery overhead without getting non-blocking behavior, and you're blocking Tomcat threads on Netty's event loop work.

Sequential auth + Influx calls in MeasurementsService
getMeasurements/getFields/getData do auth-check then Influx query, both blocking. If you keep blocking I/O, parallelizing them with CompletableFuture cuts latency roughly in half — though it also wastes Influx work when auth fails. With caching from #2, this becomes mostly irrelevant.